Neovim Instructions
==============================

OPENING A C++ CMAKE PROJECT
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

First-time setup (once per project, or after changing CMakeLists.txt):

    cd /path/to/your/project
    cmake -B build -DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON
    ln -sf build/compile_commands.json .
    nvim .

The compile_commands.json symlink in the project root is required for
clangd to understand include paths, flags, and provide code intelligence.

To always generate compile_commands.json, add to your shell rc:

    export CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON

Then only the symlink is needed after cmake -B build.

Day-to-day:

    cd /path/to/project
    nvim .              # opens file tree
    nvim src/main.cpp   # opens a specific file directly

Building from inside nvim:

    :set makeprg=cmake\ --build\ build
    Alt+B               # runs :make, errors go to diagnostics

    For a multi-config build:
    :set makeprg=cmake\ --build\ build\ --config\ Release

OPENING A PYTHON PROJECT
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    cd /path/to/project
    nvim .

Pyright picks up the project automatically. If you use a virtualenv:

    source venv/bin/activate
    nvim .

Pyright will detect the active virtualenv. For explicit config, create
pyrightconfig.json in the project root:

    {
        "venvPath": ".",
        "venv": "venv"
    }


